
(verb :name "last-error" :std "Motoi" :library "exception" :tier "core" :kind "rd" :signature "(last-error) -> string or unset-sentinel"
  :summary "The message of the most recent error the REPL caught. Motoi-own, REPL-only: under `motoi run` it is never set."
  :explanation "A convenience for the interactive session and nothing more. When you type something at the REPL that fails, the REPL prints the error AND stashes its message text, so the next thing you type can reach for it — this is what makes `(fix)` with no argument have something to work on. Two limits decide everything about how you may use it. First, only the REPL fills the slot: `motoi run` catches nothing on your behalf, and neither does with-exception-handler, so in a script last-error stays unset no matter how many conditions you raise and catch. Second, what it returns when it IS set is the message STRING, not the condition object — there are no irritants and no type to read off it. And what it returns when unset is worse than either: a sentinel that is not any Scheme value at all. It answers #f to null?, pair?, string?, boolean? and symbol?, it prints as `()` at the top level and as nothing under display, and the engine's own error text calls it \"nothing\". There is no safe predicate for it, so the only reliable test is `(string? (last-error))` — #t means an error was recorded, #f means the slot is untouched."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(last-error)" :note "() — the unset slot, printed at the top level. Under `motoi run` this is the only answer you will ever see.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(string? (last-error))" :note "#f — nothing has been recorded, and string? is the honest way to ask.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(null? (last-error))" :note "#f — the unset value PRINTS like the empty list and is not the empty list. Do not test it with null?.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (null? (last-error)) (pair? (last-error)) (string? (last-error)) (boolean? (last-error)) (symbol? (last-error)))" :note "(#f #f #f #f #f) — every type predicate says no. The unset sentinel is outside the Scheme value space.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define last-seen 'none)\n(define (watched thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (set! last-seen (error-object-message e)) 'recovered) thunk))\n(display (list (watched (lambda () (error \"tile out of range\" 99))) last-seen))\n(newline)" :note "(recovered tile out of range) — the portable replacement: record it yourself in the handler, because last-error will not."))
  :caveats ("Motoi-own. Nothing in R7RS-small corresponds; portable code cannot use it." "Only the REPL writes the slot (src/repl.js:829 and :865). Under `motoi run` last-error is ALWAYS unset, however many conditions the script raises and catches." "with-exception-handler does NOT record anything here. Catching an error and then reading last-error still gives the unset sentinel." "The unset value is not a Scheme value. (null? (last-error)) is #f, (pair? …) is #f, (string? …) is #f, and so are boolean? and symbol? — yet it auto-prints as () and displays as nothing." "Passing the unset value to a list verb raises: (length (last-error)) reports \"length: expected a proper list, got nothing\"." "When it IS set it holds the message STRING only — no condition object, so no error-object-irritants and no error-object-type." "Test it with (string? (last-error)) and nothing else.")
  :usecases ("At the REPL, feeding the last failure to (fix) without retyping it." "At the REPL, reading the message of the thing that just went wrong." "As a worked example of why a handler should record what it catches instead of relying on ambient state.")
  :related ("error-object-message" "error-object-type" "with-exception-handler" "error" "inspect")
  :learn (:concept "Ambient state that only one host fills is a convenience, not an interface — the portable move is to record what you catch, at the moment you catch it." :prerequisites ("with-exception-handler" "error-object-message" "string?") :progression "Read the unset value and see that no type predicate claims it, then write a handler that stashes the message in your own variable, then compare: yours works under run, last-error does not.")
  :provenance "Motoi-own; nothing in R7RS-small corresponds. Implementation lib/ai/llm.js:719, slot written only by src/repl.js:829 and :865; measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (error-since-boot?)\n  (string? (last-error)))\n(display (list 'run-mode (error-since-boot?)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list (last-error) (last-error) (last-error)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (own-last-error)\n  (let ((slot 'none))\n    (list (lambda () slot) (lambda (thunk) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (set! slot (error-object-message e)) 'caught) thunk)))))\n(define pair (own-last-error))\n((cadr pair) (lambda () (error \"boom\" 7)))\n(display ((car pair)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A cart wants to show the last thing that went wrong on a debug overlay. Candidates: read last-error (one call, no plumbing — and always empty under run), keep a module-level variable that the handler writes (three lines, works everywhere), or pass the condition down to whatever draws the overlay (most explicit, most threading)."
     :explanation "eng: under run, string? answers #f, which is the whole verdict on the first candidate. math: three reads of an unset slot render as nothing at all inside a list — even the printed shape gives you no handle. cs: the second candidate written out, a reader and a recorder over one slot, and it does hold the message."
     :meta "Reach for your own slot and NOT last-error whenever the code will run outside the REPL. last-error is not wrong, it is not yours: the REPL owns the write, so a script that reads it is reading somebody else's variable and finding it empty.")
    (:eng "A debug overlay fed by ambient state shows nothing and gives no hint why; one fed by a handler shows the message." :math "Reading a slot nobody writes is a constant, and a constant on a debug display is worse than a blank one." :cs "Same shape as errno versus a returned error value: the ambient version is convenient in a shell and a hazard in a library."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A value that reads as 'nothing here yet', and the trouble that starts when 'nothing' is spelled the same as a real value."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "exception" :code "(last-error)")
       (:domain "a blank line on a form versus a line filled in with a dash" :code "(display (list 'unset-is-not-empty-list (null? (last-error))))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the empty set versus the symbol for 'undefined'" :code "(display (inspect (last-error)))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A blank box and a box containing a dash look similar on paper and mean different things: one was never filled in, the other was answered 'none'. The empty set is a set; 'undefined' is not a set at all. The unset last-error prints exactly like the empty list and answers #f to null?, so the printed form and the real identity disagree — which is the single most expensive way for a value to be spelled."
     :meta "The lesson generalises past this verb: a sentinel must be distinguishable by a PREDICATE, not by how it prints. `inspect` is the only thing in Motoi that tells the truth about this value, and it says \"null\", a word with no other meaning in the language.")
    (:eng "Two states that render identically will be confused by whoever reads the render, every time." :math "Absent and empty are different, and collapsing them loses information you cannot get back." :cs "A null that impersonates the empty list is the classic sentinel bug; the fix is a predicate, not a nicer printer."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (list (null? (last-error)) (pair? (last-error)) (string? (last-error)) (boolean? (last-error)) (symbol? (last-error))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list 'stable (eq? (last-error) (last-error)) 'equals-empty-list (equal? (last-error) '())))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'raised (error-object-message e))) (lambda () (length (last-error)))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "Under `motoi run` the slot is never written, and the value it holds belongs to no Scheme type: five type predicates all answer #f, it is not equal? to the empty list it prints as, and handing it to a list verb raises."
     :invariant "Under run: for every type predicate p in {null?, pair?, string?, boolean?, symbol?}, p(last-error()) = #f; and last-error() is eq? to itself but not equal? to '()"
     :meta "The third program is the one that settles it. length raises rather than answering, and the message the engine chooses is \"expected a proper list, got nothing\" — the engine has its own word for this value and that word is nothing. What would falsify the claim: any predicate answering #t, or the slot holding a string after a caught error, which it does not — with-exception-handler never writes it."
    )
    (:eng "Five predicates and a raise is the cheapest complete description of a value with no type." :math "Not equal? to '() while printing as () is the exact statement that the printer and the identity disagree." :cs "eq? to itself confirms it is one stable object, so this is a sentinel, not a fresh value per call."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define last-seen 'none)\n(define (watched thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (set! last-seen (error-object-message e)) 'recovered) thunk))\n(display (list (watched (lambda () (error \"tile out of range\" 99))) last-seen))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define log '())\n(define (record thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (set! log (cons (error-object-type e) log)) 'next) thunk))\n(record (lambda () (escalate 'a)))\n(record (lambda () (escalate 'b)))\n(display (reverse log))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (with-last-error)\n  (let ((slot #f))\n    (cons (lambda () slot)\n          (lambda (thunk) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (set! slot (error-object-message e)) #f) thunk)))))\n(define box (with-last-error))\n(display (list 'before ((car box)) 'result ((cdr box) (lambda () (error \"disk\" 1))) 'after ((car box))))\n(newline)"
     :base "last-error is a slot only the REPL writes, holding only a message string."
     :emergent "eng: your own last-seen, one variable and one set! inside the handler, and it works under run. math: a LOG rather than a slot — keep every failure's type, not just the newest, and the failures become a sequence you can read. cs: the slot closed over, so the reader and the recorder ship together and nothing else can write it."
     :meta "Each composition is strictly better than the verb it replaces, and the third is the one to keep: closing the slot over a lambda gives you the same one-call convenience with none of the ambient reach, and #f is a real Scheme value that string? and boolean? both have opinions about. If you find yourself wanting last-error in a script, build this instead — it is six lines and it does not lie about being empty."
    )
    (:eng "One variable and one set! is the entire cost of not depending on the host." :math "A log generalises a slot: the slot is the log truncated to length one, and truncation is a choice you should make on purpose." :cs "Closing over the slot makes the write site enumerable — exactly what ambient state gives up."))
)
